1678	\begin{definition}[Directly indexed sub-line laws]
1679		\label{def:direct-subline-law}
1680		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SubLineWitness}
1681		\leanok
1682		\uses{def:ith-restricted-line, def:line-representative, def:combine-map}
1683		Put $n=2m+2$. The directly indexed extended line-point law samples
1684		$i\in\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and $u,v\in\F_q^n$ independently and uniformly,
1685		and chooses the axis or diagonal kind with equal probabilities.
1686		For the axis kind set $w=e_i$; for the diagonal kind set $w_j=0$ for
1687		$j<i$ and $w_j=v_j$ for $j\ge i$. The line has base $L_w^{\mathrm{lnf}}(u)$
1688		and direction $w$, retaining its kind and coordinate index; the sampled
1689		point is $u$. Zero diagonal directions are included.
1690	
1691		A directly indexed sub-line law $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ is a probability law
1692		on triples $(\ell,\ell_X,\ell_Z)$ with the following properties.
1693		Its extended-line marginal is the line marginal just defined.
1694		Every supported triple admits affine maps $t\mapsto a_W+b_Wt$ such that
1695		\[
1696			\pi_W(\ell(t))=\ell_W(a_W+b_Wt),\qquad W\in\{X,Z\},
1697		\]
1698		where $\ell(t)$ denotes base plus parameter times direction and the
1699		field identifications are understood. Thus each extended point projects
1700		onto its two source lines, with affine scalar coordinates $\alpha(t)$
1701		and $\beta(t)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:combine-map}. An axis-parallel
1702		extended line has axis-parallel source lines.
1703	
1704		Finally there is one probability law on
1705		$(\mathrm v,i,j)\in\{\mathrm{ALine},\mathrm{DLine}\}
1706		\times\{0,\dots,m-1\}^2$ with the following two mixture properties.
1707		Sample a triple from $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ and an independent uniform
1708		parameter $t$ on $\ell$. The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_X(\ell(t)))$
1709		is the mixture obtained by sampling $(\ell_X,x)$ from
1710		$D_{\mathrm v,i}$ and independently $\ell_Z$ from the line marginal of
1711		$D_{\mathrm v,j}$, and retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,x)$.
1712		The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_Z(\ell(t)))$ is the mixture obtained
1713		by retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,z)$ from the corresponding independent
1714		samples of $\ell_X$ and $(\ell_Z,z)$. These are separate one-point
1715		marginal identities, with the same mixture weights; they impose no
1716		joint conditional independence of the two projected points.
1717		Identification with the source law $D$ is a separate obligation;
1718		see \cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
1719	\end{definition}
